From: shirogane <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] riscv: sunxi: add the RISC-V platform glue for D1

Upstream already carries everything D1-specific below the platform line --
clk_d1, dram_sun20i_d1, the pinctrl and SPI variants, the USB PHY, and the
whole sun20i device tree set in dts/upstream. What is missing is the sunxi
platform layer itself on RISC-V: all of it lives in arch/arm/mach-sunxi/
Kconfig behind ARCH_SUNXI, which a RISC-V build never sources.

Rather than move that Kconfig somewhere shared -- which would touch every
ARM sunxi defconfig -- add board/sunxi/Kconfig.riscv, sourced only from
arch/riscv/Kconfig, declaring BOARD_SUNXI as the RISC-V counterpart of
ARCH_SUNXI. Symbols common to both are simply declared in both places;
Kconfig merges same-named symbols as long as the types agree.

board-riscv.c is the board layer: eGON/TOC0 header parsing, boot source
detection, DRAM bring-up from the SPL, early PMIC setup, the handover back
to FEL, environment placement and FIT configuration matching.

The start.S changes are fifteen lines of call sites. The assembly itself
lives in asm/arch-sunxi/fel.h behind four macros that expand to nothing off
sunxi, so qemu, th1520 and k230 are unaffected -- an unconditional early
UART init hardcoding D1's UART3 would have broken all of them. Both the
early UART and the FEL handover are Kconfig-gated, and the macros
additionally require XPL_BUILD so that U-Boot proper carries neither
fel_stash nor return_to_fel.

The SPL stack goes to 0x40000 rather than the default 0x48000: the BROM
keeps its FEL state in 0x40000-0x48000 (xfel reports the scratchpad at
0x45000). That needs SPL_HAVE_INIT_STACK, which is why common/spl/Kconfig
lets BOARD_SUNXI take the SPL_SHARES_INIT_SP_ADDR=n path -- otherwise the
SPL would use CUSTOM_SYS_INIT_SP_ADDR, which points into DRAM that does not
exist yet when the SPL runs.

No Linux device trees are copied. scripts/Makefile.lib puts
dts/upstream/src/riscv/allwinner/ on dtc's include path unconditionally, so
a local board dts resolves sun20i-d1s.dtsi from the submodule. The file is
named sunxi-u-boot.dtsi so that CONFIG_SYS_SOC="sunxi" makes
u_boot_dtsi_options pick it up automatically.

board_usb_init() is here for the same reason. musb-new registers its gadget
from the driver's probe, driver model only probes on demand, and with
DM_USB_GADGET off the only thing udc_device_get_by_index() has to probe with
is board_usb_init() -- which board/sunxi/board.c never needed because ARM
gets there another way. Without it "dfu" fails with "Controller
uninitialized". Turning DM_USB_GADGET on is not the alternative: musb_register()
only handles MUSB_PERIPHERAL while it is off.

spl_spinand_sunxi.c is the other half of being able to boot at all. The BROM
reads the SPL out of a SPI NAND, but nothing in U-Boot can read the *next*
stage from one: spl_spi.c probes for a JEDEC SPI NOR, and spl_spi_sunxi.c is
NOR-only and ARM-only. Boards whose only medium is SPI NAND -- which the D1s
designs are -- stop dead in the SPL without it. The register sequences follow
Allwinner's own boot0, minus its sample-delay calibration (running the bus
from the 24MHz oscillator lands in the case where there is nothing to tune)
and minus quad/dual reads and two-plane addressing.

env_get_location() gains ENVL_MTD for the same reason: SPI NAND arrives as
BOOT_DEVICE_NAND but is reached through the MTD stack, and without a match
there the function returns ENVL_UNKNOWN, which board_f() cannot recover from.
---
